<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nannus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nannus was, according to the foundation legend of Massalia, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segobrigii">Segobrigii</a>, a people living near the site of Marseille. In the story told by the Roman historian Justin, he welcomed Greek settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a>, and when his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyptis">Gyptis</a> chose their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protis">Protis</a> as her husband, he gave them land on which to found the city around 600 BCE.</p></div>
